rmsnorm/nki/n28                 1.626    0.0581     188.8      6.74     1.07x   16.2%
rmsnorm/torch/n28               0.636    0.0227       6.4      0.23     0.04x    1.4%

ATTRIBUTION — fixed vs marginal traffic
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Traffic is NOT linear in N: a small NEFF carries fixed setup traffic that dominates
  at N=1. Dividing total traffic by N therefore overstates per-call cost at small N and
  produces a false 'kernel is inefficient' reading. With two call counts we can solve
  for both terms instead:
      traffic(N) = FIXED + N * MARGINAL
  MARGINAL is the real per-call cost, and it is what should be compared to the floor.

  silu/nki:  traffic(1)=18.87 MB, traffic(28)=188.74 MB
    marginal per call =   6.29 MB = 1.00x the unfused floor
    fixed per NEFF    =  12.58 MB = 4.0 tiles of setup

  silu/torch:  traffic(1)=6.29 MB, traffic(28)=6.29 MB
    marginal per call =   0.00 MB = 0.00x the unfused floor
    fixed per NEFF    =   6.29 MB = 2.0 tiles of setup

  rmsnorm/nki:  traffic(1)=18.88 MB, traffic(28)=188.76 MB
    marginal per call =   6.29 MB = 1.00x the unfused floor
    fixed per NEFF    =  12.59 MB = 4.0 tiles of setup

  rmsnorm/torch:  traffic(1)=7.88 MB, traffic(28)=6.42 MB
    marginal per call =  -0.05 MB = -0.01x the unfused floor
    fixed per NEFF    =   7.93 MB = 2.5 tiles of setup

VERDICT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  silu:
    device time at N=28:  NKI   0.609 ms vs torch   0.224 ms = 2.72x
    marginal traffic/call:   NKI   6.29 MB vs torch   0.00 MB
    NKI vs unfused floor:    1.00x
    -> NKI marginal traffic is EXACTLY the unfused floor (one read in, one
       write out). The kernel spills nothing and is optimal for an op that
       cannot fuse. Kernel quality is NOT the problem.
    -> torch marginal traffic is 0.00 MB, far below the floor, which is
       only possible if the chain FUSED into a single pass.
    => The entire device gap is the FUSION BARRIER.

  rmsnorm:
    device time at N=28:  NKI   1.626 ms vs torch   0.636 ms = 2.56x
    marginal traffic/call:   NKI   6.29 MB vs torch  -0.05 MB
    NKI vs unfused floor:    1.00x
    -> NKI marginal traffic is EXACTLY the unfused floor (one read in, one
       write out). The kernel spills nothing and is optimal for an op that
       cannot fuse. Kernel quality is NOT the problem.
    -> torch marginal traffic is -0.05 MB, far below the floor, which is
       only possible if the chain FUSED into a single pass.
    => The entire device gap is the FUSION BARRIER.

====================================================================================================
WHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE RECOMMENDATION
====================================================================================================
  A NKI custom call is opaque to the Neuron compiler, so it cannot fuse across one.
  Replacing a torch op with a NKI kernel therefore does not merely add dispatch cost —
  it REMOVES a fusion opportunity the compiler was already exploiting. For
  memory-bound ops (elementwise activations, normalisations) fusion is the entire
  optimisation, so the swap loses on device even with a perfect kernel.

  That is a property of the per-layer swap model, not of NKI or of these kernels, and
  it is why the ops best suited to the Kernel Hub mechanism are the ones LEAST suited
  to benefit from it.

